WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.920 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.350 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:13.230 align:middle line:90%


00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.510 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
first some review.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.270 align:middle line:84%
Last time, we were
doing "unlockable,"

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.300 align:middle line:84%
and I was trying to
convince you that it's

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.910 align:middle line:84%
useful to think of words,
at least some words,

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.070 align:middle line:90%
as consisting of multiple parts.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.590 align:middle line:84%
We're calling these
parts morphemes.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:33.850 align:middle line:84%
And there's this process
of combining morphemes,

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.830 align:middle line:84%
we called it merge,
where you take

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.110 align:middle line:84%
two things, two
morphemes-- not necessarily

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.728 align:middle line:84%
two morphemes, two things,
and you put them together

00:00:40.728 --> 00:00:41.770 align:middle line:90%
and you make a new thing.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.080 align:middle line:84%
So you can take a
morpheme like "un-"

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.080 align:middle line:84%
and a morpheme like "lock,"
and you can put them together

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.810 align:middle line:90%
to make a verb "unlock."

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.320 align:middle line:90%
And so I just corrected myself.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.420 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes the two things
you take are morphemes,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.280 align:middle line:84%
but sometimes they
are the results

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.020 align:middle line:90%
of previous instances of merge.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.360 align:middle line:84%
So you can also take
this thing "unlock"

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.050 align:middle line:84%
that you've made as a result
of merging "un-" and "lock,"

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.080 align:middle line:84%
and merge that with "-able"
to get you "unlockable."

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.850 align:middle line:84%
That procedure that
I just ran through

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.650 align:middle line:84%
is what gives you
the tree on the left.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.720 align:middle line:84%
So the trees there
are meant to be

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.040 align:middle line:84%
representations of the order
in which you did things.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.280 align:middle line:84%
So the tree on the left
is a representation

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.340 align:middle line:90%
of two merge operations.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.080 align:middle line:84%
First, merging
"un-" with "lock,"

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.510 align:middle line:84%
and then merging
"-able" with "unlock,"

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:34.390 align:middle line:84%
giving you an adjective that
means a functioning lock,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.230 align:middle line:90%
able to be unlocked.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.660 align:middle line:84%
This is all stuff we
went through last time.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.820 align:middle line:84%
And this is why the word
"unlockable" is ambiguous

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.278 align:middle line:84%
because, well, there are two
ways that you can assemble it,

00:01:43.278 --> 00:01:46.180 align:middle line:84%
and the two ways give you
different interpretations.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.930 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions
about any of that?

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.390 align:middle line:84%
This is all stuff
we did last time.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:50.890 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.130 align:middle line:90%


00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:54.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:55.210 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.940 align:middle line:84%
So that's what we were
doing with "unlockable."

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.943 align:middle line:84%
And then we also said, and we
talked about this a little bit,

00:02:00.943 --> 00:02:03.610 align:middle line:84%
there need to be some statements
about what's called allomorphy.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.640 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes you don't just
peacefully put morphemes

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.490 align:middle line:84%
next to each other,
they change as a result

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.125 align:middle line:84%
of being next to
another morpheme.

00:02:12.125 --> 00:02:13.750 align:middle line:84%
The result is what's
called allomorphy.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.490 align:middle line:84%
So a morpheme can
have allomorphs,

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.520 align:middle line:84%
different forms that it has,
depending on its environment.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.490 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes our
statements of allomorphy

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.310 align:middle line:84%
will have to be very specific
to the morphemes in question.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:29.260 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say that when you merge
"go," together with past tense,

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.760 align:middle line:90%
that the result is "went."

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.310 align:middle line:84%
And that's not the result
of a general process, that's

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.500 align:middle line:84%
something you just have to
look up when you look up

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.620 align:middle line:90%
the word "go" in English.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.010 align:middle line:84%
Or that when you add "-ity"
to something ending in "-ic,"

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.400 align:middle line:84%
like "electric," that the
"k" sound at the end of "-ic"

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.150 align:middle line:90%
will be pronounced like an "s."

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.800 align:middle line:84%
You get "electricity"
and not "electric-ity."

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.600 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes these
are statements

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.840 align:middle line:84%
about particular morphemes and
what they do when they combine.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.750 align:middle line:84%
Other times, we'll be
able to do allomorphy

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.110 align:middle line:90%
via more general rules.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.980 align:middle line:84%
So we did spend some
time talking about Polish

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.900 align:middle line:84%
where we convinced
ourselves that it's

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.820 align:middle line:84%
useful to think that
Polish has a general rule--

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.740 align:middle line:84%
which we'll circle back and
talk about more later on--

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.360 align:middle line:84%
a general rule that
says that if you

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.270 align:middle line:84%
have a "g" at the end of
a word, it becomes a "k."

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:20.800 align:middle line:84%
So in Polish, there
are various words

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.410 align:middle line:84%
that when you pronounce them,
they seem to end in "k,"

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.960 align:middle line:84%
and then we saw that if you add
the suffix so that the "k" is

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.970 align:middle line:84%
no longer at the end
of a word, sometimes

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.340 align:middle line:84%
it really was a "k," in
which case it stays a "k."

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.460 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes it was a "g"
concealing itself as a "k,"

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:37.060 align:middle line:84%
and it reveals its true self as
a "g" when you add the suffix.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.820 align:middle line:84%
Yep, this is a crash
course in Polish.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.690 align:middle line:84%
Now you know as much
about Polish as I do,

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.750 align:middle line:90%
which is not much.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.340 align:middle line:90%
Any-- this is all review.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.450 align:middle line:90%
Does this all make sense?

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.490 align:middle line:90%
No questions?

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:49.030 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:53.750 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things that
we did several times--

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.690 align:middle line:84%
in fact, I casually did
it on the last slide--

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.360 align:middle line:84%
was to use words like
"noun" and "adjective."

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.210 align:middle line:84%
And I thought we should
just take a second

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:05.500 align:middle line:84%
to talk about what we mean
when we say words like that.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.840 align:middle line:84%
I bet that a lot
of you were taught

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.430 align:middle line:90%
what nouns and adjectives are.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.360 align:middle line:84%
But it's possible that
some of you were not,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.020 align:middle line:84%
and I know that some of you are
not native speakers of English,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.860 align:middle line:84%
and this might not be high
on my list of words to learn,

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.649 align:middle line:84%
if I were learning
another language.

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:23.230 align:middle line:84%
Wow, my power cord just
turned into a pretzel.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:24.440 align:middle line:90%
This is pretty impressive.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.200 align:middle line:90%
I didn't know they did that.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.700 align:middle line:84%
So I wanted to just talk
briefly about that before we

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.370 align:middle line:90%
go any further into morphology.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.290 align:middle line:90%
What's a noun?

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.860 align:middle line:84%
You guys, maybe some of you
were taught what a noun is.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:39.580 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.170 align:middle line:90%
An object or a thing?

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:42.520 align:middle line:90%
An object or a thing?

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:43.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:47.020 align:middle line:84%
Anybody else taught things
like that about what nouns are?

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:47.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.290 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.250 align:middle line:84%
A signifier of a
particular matter.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.405 align:middle line:84%
A signifier of a
particular matter.

00:04:53.405 --> 00:04:55.450 align:middle line:90%
Huh, I like that.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.190 align:middle line:90%
That's a very classy definition.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:57.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.663 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:59.663 --> 00:05:00.580 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.410 align:middle line:84%
So I was taught that,
a person-- actually, I

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.285 align:middle line:84%
was taught a person,
place, or a thing, yeah.

00:05:04.285 --> 00:05:07.780 align:middle line:84%
And then later, I learned
that kids today were

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.430 align:middle line:90%
learning that it was also idea.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.990 align:middle line:84%
I guess on the idea that ideas
are not things, which, I guess,

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:13.490 align:middle line:90%
they're not.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:15.940 align:middle line:84%
So these are all
definitions of "noun"

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.280 align:middle line:84%
that make reference
to the kinds of things

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.590 align:middle line:84%
that nouns mean, right, so that
they refer to people, or ideas,

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:26.620 align:middle line:84%
or things, or-- yeah,
this is what nouns are.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.280 align:middle line:84%
And that's fine if
that works for you.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.310 align:middle line:84%
That might be the best way
for you to think about nouns.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.240 align:middle line:84%
Here's another way
of thinking about it,

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.800 align:middle line:84%
which you can use if you want
to if you're ever confused about

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.600 align:middle line:84%
whether something
is a noun or not,

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.840 align:middle line:84%
if you find yourself as you
go further in linguistics,

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.250 align:middle line:90%
every so often, you will--

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:46.441 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:05:46.441 --> 00:05:48.790 align:middle line:84%
Part of the point
of higher education

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.730 align:middle line:84%
is to make you, like, doubt
yourself and be unhappy,

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.902 align:middle line:84%
things that you were
sure that you understood,

00:05:53.902 --> 00:05:55.360 align:middle line:84%
all of a sudden,
you're like, wait,

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.370 align:middle line:84%
do I really deeply
understand that?

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:01.480 align:middle line:84%
And it's meant to undermine your
emotional security, basically,

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:04.030 align:middle line:90%
that's what education is for.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:08.320 align:middle line:84%
So here's another way of
thinking about what nouns are.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.360 align:middle line:84%
If you're making a
sentence, let's say,

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.120 align:middle line:84%
putting a sentence together
is like putting together

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.320 align:middle line:84%
any other complicated
thing with lots of parts.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.930 align:middle line:84%
Take your favorite example
of that, a jigsaw puzzle,

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.480 align:middle line:84%
or a model airplane,
or whatever,

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.130 align:middle line:84%
or IKEA furniture, if
you've ever assembled that.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.100 align:middle line:84%
If you're ever assembling
things like that,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:33.520 align:middle line:84%
they have different parts
with different shapes

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.140 align:middle line:84%
that go in different
places, right?

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.720 align:middle line:84%
If you're making a model
airplane, there are the wheels,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.540 align:middle line:84%
and the wheels go, well,
where the wheels go.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.150 align:middle line:84%
Or if you're making
an IKEA chair,

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.350 align:middle line:84%
there are various
boards, but then there

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:48.850 align:middle line:90%
are also screws and-- right?

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:50.392 align:middle line:84%
There are things
that you're supposed

00:06:50.392 --> 00:06:52.180 align:middle line:90%
to put in particular places.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:55.680 align:middle line:84%
And you could name those
things by their shapes, right?

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.160 align:middle line:84%
So you could talk about
the wheel on an airplane,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:03.210 align:middle line:84%
or the edge piece
on a jigsaw puzzle.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.170 align:middle line:84%
And you can also kind of
name them by their functions.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:09.720 align:middle line:84%
If you weren't sure
what an edge piece was,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.910 align:middle line:84%
well, it would be
the piece that goes

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:14.370 align:middle line:84%
on the edge of the jigsaw
puzzle, the one that

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.110 align:middle line:90%
goes on the outside.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:20.120 align:middle line:84%
And I guess kind of
similarly, "noun"--

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:22.640 align:middle line:84%
when we say that something
is a noun, what we mean

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.370 align:middle line:84%
is that it's something
that goes where nouns

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:28.100 align:middle line:90%
go, kind of like an edge piece.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.360 align:middle line:84%
It's a piece that
goes on the edge.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.330 align:middle line:84%
So if you're putting
a sentence together,

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:36.050 align:middle line:84%
there are parts of the
sentence where nouns belong.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:38.750 align:middle line:84%
And noun is just a name
for the class of things

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:41.160 align:middle line:90%
that can go in those places.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.110 align:middle line:84%
So, I mean, for example,
if you can grammatically

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.840 align:middle line:84%
finish a sentence
with a single word,

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.530 align:middle line:84%
a sentence like, we are
talking about (blank),

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:58.210 align:middle line:84%
or we are talking about "the"
(blank), then it's a noun.

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:01.755 align:middle line:84%
And I'm saying this partly as
a challenge, try to show me

00:08:01.755 --> 00:08:02.380 align:middle line:90%
that I'm wrong.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:02.880 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.900 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.210 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You
don't, you're right.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.440 align:middle line:90%
So we are talking about "him."

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.500 align:middle line:84%
So "him" gets to
count as a noun.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.310 align:middle line:84%
And we would want pronouns
to be a particular subspecies

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.830 align:middle line:90%
of noun.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.260 align:middle line:84%
That might not be such
a bad result. Yes.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:23.693 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Wouldn't names
also be able to fit in that?

00:08:23.693 --> 00:08:24.610 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.920 align:middle line:90%
So we're talking about Mary.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.420 align:middle line:90%
"Mary" would be a noun.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:29.800 align:middle line:84%
And that's probably
right, "Mary"

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.130 align:middle line:90%
should probably be a noun.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:31.630 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In a sentence we
are talking about running,

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:36.026 align:middle line:84%
there's a word for
that, isn't there?

00:08:36.026 --> 00:08:37.803 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
It's a gerund, yeah.

00:08:37.803 --> 00:08:39.220 align:middle line:84%
So this is one of
the places where

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:41.303 align:middle line:84%
you're supposed to be
filled with emotional angst.

00:08:41.303 --> 00:08:42.909 align:middle line:84%
Wait, "running,"
it's got "run" in it.

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:44.030 align:middle line:90%
That looks like a verb.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.640 align:middle line:84%
But it's a verb that's
been converted into a noun

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.460 align:middle line:84%
so that we can put
it in this sentence.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:49.960 align:middle line:90%
That's what a gerund is.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.530 align:middle line:84%
It's a name for a verb that's
been turned into a noun.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good point.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:08:59.040 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.480 align:middle line:84%
So I invite you
to try to come up

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.670 align:middle line:84%
with sentences like this for
your favorite parts of speech.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:04.980 align:middle line:90%
Here's another one.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.710 align:middle line:84%
If you can grammatically finish
a sentence like, "I consider

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:11.250 align:middle line:84%
her (blank)," and the result
is a sentence, that means,

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.710 align:middle line:90%
"I think that she is (blank)."

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:18.550 align:middle line:84%
So I consider her tall,
or brilliant, or whatever,

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:19.855 align:middle line:90%
then that's an adjective.

00:09:19.855 --> 00:09:21.105 align:middle line:90%
The thing that's in the blank.

00:09:21.105 --> 00:09:24.120 align:middle line:90%


00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.030 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes the sentences will
not make a whole lot of sense.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.390 align:middle line:90%
So "I consider her autumnal"--

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:32.400 align:middle line:90%
It's not clear what that means.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.380 align:middle line:84%
But if you compare it with
"I consider her run," yeah.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.880 align:middle line:84%
So "I consider her run"
just makes no sense at all.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.910 align:middle line:84%
Whereas "I consider
her autumnal."

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.170 align:middle line:90%
I sound deeply poetic, I guess.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.790 align:middle line:84%
And you wonder what
I mean by that.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:46.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.060 align:middle line:84%
But-- so "autumnal"
is an adjective,

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.010 align:middle line:90%
"run" is not an adjective.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:52.980 align:middle line:84%
And I have this bit about
that it has to mean,

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.310 align:middle line:84%
"I think she is (blank),"
because you can say things

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:58.560 align:middle line:90%
like, "I consider her often."

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.630 align:middle line:84%
That means "I think
about her often."

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.760 align:middle line:84%
"Often" is not an
adjective, because if I

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.590 align:middle line:84%
say I consider
her often, I don't

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.450 align:middle line:90%
mean I think that she is often.

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:07.020 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.300 align:middle line:90%
That's not what that means.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.750 align:middle line:90%
So "often" is not an adjective.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:13.230 align:middle line:84%
It's an adverb, actually,
and we'll talk about that.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:14.958 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to make
you go through this

00:10:14.958 --> 00:10:16.500 align:middle line:84%
for all the parts
of speech, but this

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:17.520 align:middle line:90%
is an interesting exercise.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:18.030 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.875 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this the
answer to our problem?

00:10:19.875 --> 00:10:21.450 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Uh-huh.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:22.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:26.125 align:middle line:84%
It's also [INAUDIBLE],, I
think it's coming [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:26.125 --> 00:10:27.750 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I
consider her coming.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.600 align:middle line:90%


00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.380 align:middle line:90%
I'm full of doubt and angst.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.040 align:middle line:84%
So there is a parse of that,
I consider her coming, which

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:41.820 align:middle line:84%
means, I am thinking about
the fact that she is coming.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.490 align:middle line:90%
It can mean that, I think.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:48.825 align:middle line:84%
I consider her-- can
it mean I consid--

00:10:48.825 --> 00:10:50.265 align:middle line:90%
I believe that she is coming?

00:10:50.265 --> 00:10:52.832 align:middle line:90%


00:10:52.832 --> 00:10:55.040 align:middle line:84%
I know there are other
English speakers in this room.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:55.940 align:middle line:90%
What do you guys think?

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:56.440 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I know there's like
that one part of the game not

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:10.833 align:middle line:90%
really ever [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:10.833 --> 00:11:11.750 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.151 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:13.151 --> 00:11:16.887 align:middle line:90%


00:11:16.887 --> 00:11:20.830 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, yeah.

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:21.330 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:25.083 align:middle line:90%
So-- but I think--

00:11:25.083 --> 00:11:26.250 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry, what's your name?

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:27.170 align:middle line:90%
Is it Ivana?

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:28.050 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:29.700 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, what is
your-- what is your--

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.010 align:middle line:84%
you were asking about
"I consider her coming"

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.155 align:middle line:90%
just a second ago.

00:11:33.155 --> 00:11:33.655 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:33.655 --> 00:11:34.488 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, Vlada.

00:11:34.488 --> 00:11:37.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry,
Vlada, I got you wrong, sorry.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:39.270 align:middle line:84%
Vlada is asking
about whether it's

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:43.710 align:middle line:84%
possible to have a form like
"coming" as an adjective here.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.940 align:middle line:84%
Forms like "coming"
can be adjectives.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:48.480 align:middle line:84%
So you can say things
like "the coming century,"

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.970 align:middle line:84%
right, where "coming" is
being used as an adjective.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:56.910 align:middle line:84%
So I said a second ago,
"running" can be a noun, right?

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.830 align:middle line:90%
We're talking about "running."

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:02.280 align:middle line:84%
This is one of many tricky
things about English.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.670 align:middle line:84%
Forms of verbs that end
in "-ing" can be nouns,

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:07.080 align:middle line:90%
and then we call them gerunds.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.170 align:middle line:84%
They can also be
participles, and then we

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.180 align:middle line:90%
call them adjectives.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:13.883 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, they can
also be adjectives,

00:12:13.883 --> 00:12:15.300 align:middle line:84%
and then we call
them participles.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:16.367 align:middle line:90%
Let me do that again.

00:12:16.367 --> 00:12:18.450 align:middle line:84%
"-ing," if you add it to
a noun, there's an "-ing"

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:21.150 align:middle line:84%
that you add to a noun that
makes it into a-- sorry.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.400 align:middle line:84%
There's an "-ing" that you
add to a verb that makes it

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.690 align:middle line:90%
into a noun.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:27.150 align:middle line:84%
That's when it's a
gerund, like in "We're

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:28.110 align:middle line:90%
talking about running."

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.360 align:middle line:84%
There's an "-ing" that you
add to a verb that makes it

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.580 align:middle line:84%
into an adjective, and
that's a present participle.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.538 align:middle line:84%
That's the kind of thing
Vlada is asking about.

00:12:34.538 --> 00:12:37.260 align:middle line:84%
So "the coming century,"
or "a running man,"

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:41.010 align:middle line:84%
those 'running"s are-- those
"-ing" verbs are adjectives,

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.080 align:middle line:90%
they're participles.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.450 align:middle line:84%
Sort of like, if
we go back to "un-"

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.265 align:middle line:84%
we convinced
ourselves that "un-"

00:12:47.265 --> 00:12:51.840 align:middle line:84%
has a couple of different
forms, that it can attach either

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.240 align:middle line:84%
to an adjective, or
to a verb, right,

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.620 align:middle line:84%
with slightly
different meanings.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:00.360 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, "-ing" has
several different guises.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.160 align:middle line:84%
It can be a couple
of different things.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.050 align:middle line:84%
English really needs
more morphemes.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.982 align:middle line:84%
We have several morphemes that
are pronounced the same way.

00:13:06.982 --> 00:13:08.190 align:middle line:90%
Not every language does that.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:09.607 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty
of languages that

00:13:09.607 --> 00:13:13.890 align:middle line:84%
have just different forms
for gerunds and participles.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:14.410 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:14.910 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.440 align:middle line:84%
So this has just
been an exercise

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.780 align:middle line:84%
in trying to convince ourselves
that we know what we're talking

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:19.860 align:middle line:84%
about when we're
talking about things

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.270 align:middle line:90%
like nouns and adjectives.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.060 align:middle line:84%
And like I say, I
won't make you do

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.100 align:middle line:90%
this for every part of speech.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.990 align:middle line:84%
But if you want to
annoy your friends back

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:35.070 align:middle line:84%
in the dorm, trying to come
up with sentences like this

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:36.880 align:middle line:84%
might be an
interesting exercise.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:38.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:41.270 align:middle line:84%
Interesting for me,
maybe not for them.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:41.770 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.880 align:middle line:90%
Questions about any of that?

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:45.830 align:middle line:84%
This has just been
so that if anybody

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:47.930 align:middle line:84%
heard all this talk about
nouns and adjectives

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.815 align:middle line:84%
and was wondering what the heck
are we talking about exactly,

00:13:50.815 --> 00:13:52.565 align:middle line:84%
that is what we are
talking about exactly.

00:13:52.565 --> 00:13:55.760 align:middle line:90%


00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:59.570 align:middle line:84%
So we've been talking now about
morphology for a little while.

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:03.740 align:middle line:84%
And we have mostly been
talking about language

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:05.310 align:middle line:90%
specific properties.

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:09.620 align:middle line:84%
So we've been saying a
morpheme with a given meaning

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:11.730 align:middle line:84%
is pronounced differently
in different languages,

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:12.980 align:middle line:90%
so we started with cats.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.410 align:middle line:84%
We convinced
ourselves that if we

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:20.870 align:middle line:84%
wanted to look for things that
were universal about language,

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:22.520 align:middle line:84%
figuring out that
the word for cat

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.280 align:middle line:84%
is "cat," that's
clearly not universal.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.280 align:middle line:84%
Every language has a
different word for cat.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.380 align:middle line:84%
So morphemes with
a different meaning

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:34.760 align:middle line:84%
can be pronounced differently
in different languages.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:38.220 align:middle line:90%
Morphemes can be various types.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:39.980 align:middle line:84%
They can be prefixes,
or suffixes,

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:41.730 align:middle line:90%
or infixes, or other things.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:46.580 align:middle line:84%
So those here are three verbs
in English, Lardil, and Tagalog,

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:48.800 align:middle line:90%
that all mean "danced."

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.470 align:middle line:84%
They all have--
consist of a verb,

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:54.050 align:middle line:84%
along with another morpheme
that indicates past tense.

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:55.700 align:middle line:84%
That morpheme is a
suffix in English,

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:59.930 align:middle line:84%
but it's a prefix in Lardil,
and it's an infix in Tagalog.

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:03.320 align:middle line:84%
And morphemes can
be bound or free.

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:06.390 align:middle line:84%
So here are some new
examples of that.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.640 align:middle line:84%
This is, again, a point of
cross-linguistic variation.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.450 align:middle line:84%
So in English, a phrase
like "in my hand"

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:16.130 align:middle line:84%
consists of three words,
three free morphemes.

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:21.540 align:middle line:84%
But in Turkish, it consists of
a root plus two bound morphemes.

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.890 align:middle line:84%
So there's the word
for hand, to which you

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:28.050 align:middle line:84%
are adding suffixes that mean
things like "my" and "in."

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:28.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:30.150 align:middle line:90%
Anybody here speak Turkish?

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:30.650 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:31.400 align:middle line:90%
Is this true?

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.120 align:middle line:90%
Am I lying?

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.040 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:36.950 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, in
English, a sentence

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:40.520 align:middle line:84%
like "I bought a bed" consists
of four free standing words.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:43.200 align:middle line:84%
In Mohawk, it
consists of one word.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:46.470 align:middle line:84%
So there's a verb "buy" that is
combining with various things,

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:48.800 align:middle line:84%
including a morpheme
that means "bed."

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:52.530 align:middle line:84%
So the object becomes
part of this long verb.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.600 align:middle line:90%
Anybody here speak Mohawk?

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:55.100 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:56.450 align:middle line:90%
So please just believe me, yeah.

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:58.310 align:middle line:90%
This is how Mohawk works.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:00.830 align:middle line:84%
This is a process called
noun incorporation.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:03.020 align:middle line:84%
It's not obligatory in
Mohawk, but it's an option

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:05.360 align:middle line:90%
that you have.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:06.830 align:middle line:84%
So languages can
vary with respect

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:10.100 align:middle line:84%
to how their morphemes
are pronounced,

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:12.780 align:middle line:84%
whether they're prefixes or
suffixes, or other things,

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:16.310 align:middle line:84%
and whether they
are bound or free.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:19.700 align:middle line:84%
Languages are sometimes
informally classified

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:23.450 align:middle line:84%
by how likely their
morphemes are to be bound.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:27.650 align:middle line:84%
And when I say that
they're informally

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:29.630 align:middle line:84%
classified in this
way, what I mean is,

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:34.907 align:middle line:84%
it's probably hopeless to try to
tightly define what this means.

00:16:34.907 --> 00:16:36.740 align:middle line:84%
But this is a quick and
dirty classification

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:38.030 align:middle line:90%
that you will hear people use.

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:39.650 align:middle line:90%
There are languages out there--

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:43.820 align:middle line:84%
Mandarin is the poster child
for this-- that just are not

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:45.390 align:middle line:90%
into bound morphemes.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.360 align:middle line:84%
So all of the
morphemes are free.

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.480 align:middle line:84%
They don't have to be
attached to anything.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.960 align:middle line:84%
So in Chinese, "he
ate the meal" consists

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:59.450 align:middle line:84%
of four free standing
words, doesn't have prefixes

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:01.280 align:middle line:90%
or suffixes, mostly.

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:03.440 align:middle line:84%
There are a few
things in Mandarin

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:06.060 align:middle line:84%
that people argue about whether
they might be suffixes or not.

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:08.900 align:middle line:84%
So like you can
make pronouns plural

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.000 align:middle line:90%
by adding a "men" after them.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.510 align:middle line:84%
So "wo" is "I" and
"women" is "we."

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.329 align:middle line:84%
So you add the "men" and
you get a plural version.

00:17:17.329 --> 00:17:19.540 align:middle line:84%
It's a candidate for
a bound morpheme,

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:21.290 align:middle line:84%
but it's certainly a
language that doesn't

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.690 align:middle line:90%
have a lot of bound morphemes.

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:29.030 align:middle line:84%
Opposite extreme are languages
like Mohawk or Wampanoag.

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.100 align:middle line:84%
This is the language
that is spoken

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:34.170 align:middle line:84%
by the traditional owners of a
lot of Eastern Massachusetts,

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:38.150 align:middle line:84%
including, arguably, the place
where we're standing right now.

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.160 align:middle line:84%
These languages are
called polysynthetic.

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:42.780 align:middle line:84%
They are really, really
into bound morphemes.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:46.580 align:middle line:84%
So things that would not be
bound morphemes in English

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:47.690 align:middle line:90%
are bound morphemes.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:52.370 align:middle line:84%
This is an example from a letter
that was written in the 1600s

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:57.830 align:middle line:84%
by a missionary to the
Wampanoag on Martha's Vineyard,

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:01.370 align:middle line:84%
a guy named Experience
Mayhew, because they knew

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:03.590 align:middle line:90%
how to give names in the 16th--

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:05.330 align:middle line:90%
1600s.

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:07.430 align:middle line:84%
He was asked,
apparently, by the person

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:09.260 align:middle line:84%
he was writing the
letter to to give him

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:11.720 align:middle line:84%
the longest word that
he could in Wampanoag.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:14.600 align:middle line:84%
And this is what
he came up with.

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:17.660 align:middle line:84%
It means "our very
skillful mirror makers."

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:20.298 align:middle line:84%
And as you can see, it consists
of lots and lots of morphemes

00:18:20.298 --> 00:18:21.590 align:middle line:90%
all piled on top of each other.

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:24.510 align:middle line:84%
He kind of cheated by putting
"mirror" in the middle of that.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.920 align:middle line:84%
So "mirror" itself is a
morphologically complex word

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:28.520 align:middle line:90%
in Wampanoag.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.700 align:middle line:84%
It means "device for
looking at your reflection."

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:36.320 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so look, "reflection
device," [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:38.085 align:middle line:84%
is the word by
itself for "mirror."

00:18:38.085 --> 00:18:39.710 align:middle line:84%
So he sort of gave
himself a head start

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:40.970 align:middle line:84%
by putting that in the
middle of the word.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:41.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:44.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
any sort of connection

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:46.910 align:middle line:84%
between, I don't know, geography
of where the language is

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:50.710 align:middle line:84%
spoken versus whether it's the
language tends to be isolating?

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:53.750 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Which
of these things it is?

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:56.570 align:middle line:84%
So the question
was whether there's

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.840 align:middle line:84%
any connection between
geography and whether a language

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:01.850 align:middle line:90%
is isolating or polysynthetic.

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:05.580 align:middle line:84%
These things kind of run in
families, in language families.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:09.530 align:middle line:84%
So there are a bunch of
isolating Polynesian languages,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:10.970 align:middle line:90%
for example.

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:12.830 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch
of polysynthetic.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:14.960 align:middle line:90%
Wampanoag is Algonquian.

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:16.430 align:middle line:90%
Mohawk is Iroquoian.

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:19.940 align:middle line:84%
Those language families
tend to be polysynthetic.

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:23.600 align:middle line:84%
And so because
language families are

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.370 align:middle line:84%
confined to particular
places, there's

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:27.770 align:middle line:84%
some kind of connection
to where you are.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.620 align:middle line:84%
I think that might be as
far as it goes, actually.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:31.120 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:32.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does
"polysynthetic" refer

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:36.038 align:middle line:84%
to the same group of
languages as "agglutinative"?

00:19:36.038 --> 00:19:36.950 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No.

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:38.690 align:middle line:84%
"Agglutinative" means
something slightly different.

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:40.607 align:middle line:84%
We'll get to "agglutinative"
in just a second.

00:19:40.607 --> 00:19:43.020 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's a
very good question.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.580 align:middle line:90%
Other questions you want to ask?

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:47.800 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:49.930 align:middle line:84%
I think we have
"agglutinative" coming up.

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:51.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:53.470 align:middle line:84%
So this is another,
again, informal way

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.870 align:middle line:90%
of classifying languages.

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.360 align:middle line:84%
If we were having
tests in this class,

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:00.790 align:middle line:84%
I would never test you on
the meanings of these terms

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:03.250 align:middle line:84%
because they're
fairly imprecise.

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:06.340 align:middle line:84%
But they're useful for giving
people a basic idea of what

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:07.750 align:middle line:90%
you're looking at.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:11.050 align:middle line:84%
Agglutinative language is-- this
is another way people sometimes

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:12.170 align:middle line:90%
compare languages.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:13.810 align:middle line:84%
Agglutinative
languages are languages

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.090 align:middle line:84%
in which there are
morphemes-- they're typically

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:17.620 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of morphemes
that are bound,

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:21.440 align:middle line:84%
and they are easy to
separate from each other.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:25.480 align:middle line:84%
So Turkish is the poster child
for an agglutinative language.

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:28.240 align:middle line:84%
You've got a verb, and
then you have a bunch

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:29.740 align:middle line:90%
of suffixes on the verb.

00:20:29.740 --> 00:20:34.270 align:middle line:84%
And making the cut between
one suffix and the next

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:36.050 align:middle line:90%
is pretty straightforward.

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:40.060 align:middle line:84%
There aren't a lot of
fancy morphological changes

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.640 align:middle line:84%
that happen as a result
of these morphemes coming

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:44.500 align:middle line:90%
in contact with each other.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:47.740 align:middle line:84%
And each of the morphemes
has a pretty simple meaning.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:51.310 align:middle line:84%
They mean things like, each
other, or cause, or past.

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:52.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:52.600 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So allomorphy wouldn't
be the present [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:57.250 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So, not in-- yeah,

00:20:57.250 --> 00:20:59.230 align:middle line:84%
not the kind of
allomorphy that causes

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:03.220 align:middle line:84%
you to wonder where one morpheme
starts and the next begins.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.055 align:middle line:90%
So there are languages--

00:21:05.055 --> 00:21:07.180 align:middle line:84%
the polysyn-- so there are
polysynthetic languages.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:09.580 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that have
lots and lots of morphology

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:10.390 align:middle line:90%
on their verbs.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:11.980 align:middle line:90%
Navajo is a very good example.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:13.870 align:middle line:84%
We'll see some Navajo
examples later,

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:15.820 align:middle line:84%
in which there
are many morphemes

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:17.860 align:middle line:84%
but they tend to
squash into each other.

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:19.360 align:middle line:84%
And you have to do
a lot of analysis

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:22.610 align:middle line:84%
to figure out where one
starts and the next begins--

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:24.230 align:middle line:84%
where one stops and
the next begins.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:24.830 align:middle line:90%
Did you have a question?

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:25.445 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No.

00:21:25.445 --> 00:21:27.630 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, OK.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:29.790 align:middle line:90%
Further questions?

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:30.950 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:35.600 align:middle line:84%
So agglutinative and
sort of the opposite

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:37.220 align:middle line:84%
of agglutinative,
people talk about

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.290 align:middle line:84%
"fusional," or
"inflectional" languages.

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:41.870 align:middle line:84%
These are languages
that have morphemes,

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:45.590 align:middle line:84%
and the morphemes squash a bunch
of bits of meaning together

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:46.920 align:middle line:90%
in one small space.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:50.150 align:middle line:84%
So these Russian
nouns, for example,

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:55.340 align:middle line:84%
end in vowels, which indicates
the grammatical gender

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:59.570 align:middle line:84%
of the noun, and also its
number, and also its case.

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:03.710 align:middle line:84%
And you aren't going
to be able to divide

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:05.360 align:middle line:90%
that vowel into parts, right?

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:08.680 align:middle line:84%
So the "oo" at the end of
[NON-ENGLISH] means feminine

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:10.040 align:middle line:90%
and singular and accusative.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.010 align:middle line:84%
And the "uh" at the end of
[NON-ENGLISH] means feminine

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:14.330 align:middle line:90%
and plural and accusative.

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.300 align:middle line:84%
And it's hopeless to try to find
a part of that suffix that's

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:20.270 align:middle line:84%
the part that means
singular or plural, right?

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:22.180 align:middle line:90%
It's just all in one big spot.

00:22:22.180 --> 00:22:22.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.008 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this the case
with endings in Spanish?

00:22:25.008 --> 00:22:26.300 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Say it, again.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:27.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this the case
with endings in Spanish?

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:29.360 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:31.445 align:middle line:84%
You're talking about the
verb endings in Spanish?

00:22:31.445 --> 00:22:33.415 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: More like [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:33.415 --> 00:22:35.870 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:38.900 align:middle line:84%
So this is why, I think
I said-- so she's asking,

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:43.126 align:middle line:84%
is this true for Spanish nouns
which end in endings like "-os"

00:22:43.126 --> 00:22:48.320 align:middle line:84%
and "-as," right, which
indicate both gender and number

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:49.340 align:middle line:90%
sometimes.

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:50.960 align:middle line:84%
And in those
particular cases, you

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:54.380 align:middle line:84%
could try to convince yourself
that the "oh" indicates

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:57.440 align:middle line:84%
masculine, and the S
indicates plural, right?

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.290 align:middle line:84%
In lots of cases,
this is why I said

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:04.760 align:middle line:84%
we're never going to test
you on this kind of thing

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:08.000 align:middle line:84%
because this classification is
really a classification of how

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.670 align:middle line:84%
easy is it to make
divisions between morphemes

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:12.930 align:middle line:90%
and make breaks in places?

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:16.190 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is, sometimes
very easy-- that's Turkish.

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:18.380 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes impossible,
that's Russian.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:21.080 align:middle line:84%
And Spanish is
somewhere in the middle.

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:23.420 align:middle line:84%
It's more maybe for
that particular case,

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:24.920 align:middle line:84%
it's more on the
agglutinative side.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:26.587 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's a break
that you can make.

00:23:26.587 --> 00:23:30.890 align:middle line:84%
But, yeah, so asking questions
like, how easy is it?

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:32.270 align:middle line:90%
This is no way to do science.

00:23:32.270 --> 00:23:33.163 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:23:33.163 --> 00:23:33.830 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:34.330 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:35.614 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:35.614 --> 00:23:37.850 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, animate.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:38.660 align:middle line:90%
Where?

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:39.830 align:middle line:90%
Oh, here.

00:23:39.830 --> 00:23:46.940 align:middle line:84%
It stands for the fact that
Russian masculine accusatives

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:52.470 align:middle line:84%
take "ah" only if they refer
to things that are alive.

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:52.970 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:56.030 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah, that's what it means.

00:23:56.030 --> 00:23:56.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:24:00.470 align:middle line:84%
So there's syncretism in
Russian between the accusative

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:04.100 align:middle line:84%
and the genitive, I guess it
is, just for animate nouns

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:07.220 align:middle line:84%
and not for
inanimate masculines.

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:08.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:10.290 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about this?

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:14.370 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:14.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:16.865 align:middle line:84%
So I'm talking
about lots of things

00:24:16.865 --> 00:24:17.990 align:middle line:90%
that are language specific.

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:20.090 align:middle line:90%
And I just wanted to--

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:23.240 align:middle line:84%
this is going to be a
topic of conversation

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:24.470 align:middle line:90%
throughout the class.

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:27.187 align:middle line:84%
When we are looking at
things, how much time

00:24:27.187 --> 00:24:29.270 align:middle line:84%
are we spending looking
at ways in which languages

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:31.010 align:middle line:84%
are different, and
how much time are we

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:33.770 align:middle line:84%
spending trying to figure
out the basic rules,

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:36.270 align:middle line:84%
the things that all
languages have in common?

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:39.650 align:middle line:84%
Both of these are important
things for linguists to do

00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:44.390 align:middle line:84%
is try to understand both
the variation and the core

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:47.090 align:middle line:90%
of common thread that all--

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:49.570 align:middle line:84%
connects all these
languages together.

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:51.440 align:middle line:84%
I just wanted to
try to convince you

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:53.960 align:middle line:84%
that even in morphology, which
is what we're starting with,

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:59.930 align:middle line:84%
there are some things
that maybe are reliable

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:01.430 align:middle line:90%
cross-linguistically.

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:05.240 align:middle line:84%
So here's a Georgian
verb and a Turkish verb.

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:07.650 align:middle line:84%
I don't know either
of these languages

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:09.650 align:middle line:84%
so I had to pick
examples that had

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:11.250 align:middle line:90%
a different verb at the bottom.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:13.310 align:middle line:84%
So in Georgian,
you've got "paint."

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:15.440 align:middle line:84%
And in Turkish,
you've got "open."

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.520 align:middle line:84%
But otherwise, these
verbs are the same.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:20.150 align:middle line:84%
So the Georgian one means,
"I will have him paint it."

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:22.740 align:middle line:84%
And the Turkish one means,
"I had him open it."

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:23.240 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:23.780 align:middle line:90%
They aren't the same.

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:24.950 align:middle line:90%
They're different tenses.

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:27.252 align:middle line:84%
But they're both
about causing someone

00:25:27.252 --> 00:25:28.460 align:middle line:90%
to do something to something.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:30.857 align:middle line:90%
There, they have that in common.

00:25:30.857 --> 00:25:32.190 align:middle line:90%
And they have some other things.

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:33.898 align:middle line:84%
And they're different
in other ways, too.

00:25:33.898 --> 00:25:37.790 align:middle line:84%
So the Georgian verb is
preceded by several prefixes.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:42.350 align:middle line:84%
The Turkish verb is followed
by several suffixes.

00:25:42.350 --> 00:25:45.140 align:middle line:84%
But here's what they
finally have in common.

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:51.830 align:middle line:84%
The red affix is merged
after the blue affix.

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:56.720 align:middle line:84%
So the affix that means
"cause" is the first thing

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:59.420 align:middle line:84%
that's merged with the verb
in both of these languages.

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.820 align:middle line:84%
And the affix that
means first person

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:06.890 align:middle line:84%
singular, "I," as the
subject, is merged later

00:26:06.890 --> 00:26:08.060 align:middle line:90%
in both of these.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:10.820 align:middle line:84%
Now, in Georgian,
they're both prefixes.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:12.470 align:middle line:84%
In Turkish, they're
both suffixes.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:16.700 align:middle line:84%
They're not pronounced
the same way, right?

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:19.100 align:middle line:90%
"Cause" in Georgian is "a."

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:21.710 align:middle line:90%
"Cause" in Turkish is "tir."

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.350 align:middle line:84%
But they do have that
property in common,

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:28.070 align:middle line:84%
that the order in which
you combine these morphemes

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:31.310 align:middle line:84%
is the same for these two
otherwise fairly different

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:32.630 align:middle line:90%
languages.

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:35.000 align:middle line:84%
And that's a pretty
reliable thing,

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:38.510 align:middle line:84%
so what's sometimes called
derivation morphology, which

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:40.528 align:middle line:84%
includes things like
all the morphology

00:26:40.528 --> 00:26:42.320 align:middle line:84%
we were talking about,
that converts things

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:45.080 align:middle line:84%
from nouns into verbs or
from verbs into adjectives

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:48.440 align:middle line:84%
or whatever all
else, and also things

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:51.950 align:middle line:84%
like causative, that change
the number of participants

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:54.920 align:middle line:84%
in a verb, so by
adding someone who

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:57.650 align:middle line:90%
is causing a verb to happen.

00:26:57.650 --> 00:27:02.827 align:middle line:84%
That type of morphology tends
to be merged earlier than what's

00:27:02.827 --> 00:27:04.910 align:middle line:84%
sometimes called inflectional
morphology, the kind

00:27:04.910 --> 00:27:07.010 align:middle line:84%
of morphology that
indicates things like who's

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:09.830 align:middle line:90%
doing what to whom and when.

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:12.245 align:middle line:84%
That's a moderately
reliable fact.

00:27:12.245 --> 00:27:15.110 align:middle line:90%


00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:18.050 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, there
are various places

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:21.680 align:middle line:84%
where a pair of
expressions can differ

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:24.380 align:middle line:84%
in all kinds of ways, including
whether you're looking

00:27:24.380 --> 00:27:25.710 align:middle line:90%
at prefixes or suffixes.

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:28.465 align:middle line:84%
So here's Swahili for "you
hit" and German for "you put."

00:27:28.465 --> 00:27:30.590 align:middle line:84%
And abstracting away from
the fact that one of them

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:33.240 align:middle line:84%
is hitting and the other
is putting, in Swahili,

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.240 align:middle line:84%
those are prefixes, in
German, they're suffixes.

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:37.220 align:middle line:84%
But the order in which
everything is merged

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:39.350 align:middle line:90%
is the same.

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:42.860 align:middle line:84%
In English and Turkish, "in my
hands," and the Turkish version

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:49.730 align:middle line:84%
of that, "ellerimde," the
Turkish morphemes are bound,

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:51.170 align:middle line:90%
the English morphemes are free.

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:54.330 align:middle line:84%
But the morphemes are being
merged in the same order.

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:56.120 align:middle line:84%
So there's some
hope for the idea

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:59.450 align:middle line:84%
that there is some core that
these languages all share

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:01.337 align:middle line:84%
that we want to try
to understand what

00:28:01.337 --> 00:28:03.170 align:middle line:84%
is it that determines
the order in which you

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:04.533 align:middle line:90%
do your merge operations.

00:28:04.533 --> 00:28:06.950 align:middle line:84%
If you thought I was going to
answer that question for you

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:08.870 align:middle line:84%
today, then I'm about
to disappoint you.

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:11.080 align:middle line:84%
But that's a question
that we're going to want

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:12.330 align:middle line:90%
to keep in mind going forward.

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:13.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:15.258 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does this have
anything to do with the fact

00:28:15.258 --> 00:28:17.480 align:middle line:84%
that, at least in English,
there's like a certain way

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:18.980 align:middle line:84%
that you can say--
like, if you have

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:20.780 align:middle line:84%
a list of adjectives
describing a noun,

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:22.100 align:middle line:90%
you say them in a certain way.

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:25.340 align:middle line:84%
So you say like
"the big bad dog,"

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:27.170 align:middle line:90%
but not the "bad big dog?"

00:28:27.170 --> 00:28:28.378 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Bad big dog.

00:28:28.378 --> 00:28:31.242 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so that's a
very nice point.

00:28:31.242 --> 00:28:33.200 align:middle line:84%
When you have a bunch of
adjectives in English,

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:34.783 align:middle line:84%
they tend to go in
a particular order.

00:28:34.783 --> 00:28:36.260 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:39.440 align:middle line:84%
And actually, there's a
very articulated order.

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:43.280 align:middle line:84%
It's got like five slots for
the adjectives and the order

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:44.690 align:middle line:90%
that they go in.

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:47.210 align:middle line:84%
The other cool thing
about it is that

00:28:47.210 --> 00:28:51.690 align:middle line:84%
among languages that put their
adjectives before the noun,

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:55.400 align:middle line:84%
if they have any rules for the
order of the adjective, that's

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:56.690 align:middle line:90%
the rule.

00:28:56.690 --> 00:28:59.540 align:middle line:84%
So that's the only kind
of rule that there can be.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.830 align:middle line:84%
For languages that put their
adjectives after the noun,

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:05.570 align:middle line:84%
if they have any
rules for the order,

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:06.870 align:middle line:90%
there are two kinds of rules.

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:08.990 align:middle line:84%
So if you number
the adjectives that

00:29:08.990 --> 00:29:11.690 align:middle line:84%
go before the noun in English,
if you say they go in the order

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:15.380 align:middle line:84%
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, if
you're a language that

00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:17.870 align:middle line:84%
puts your adjectives
after your nouns,

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:20.090 align:middle line:84%
there are only two kinds
of languages like that.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:22.820 align:middle line:84%
There are languages in which
the order is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:24.740 align:middle line:90%
so you get noun, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:28.610 align:middle line:84%
So it'd be like you
say "house big black,"

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:31.340 align:middle line:84%
and you can't say
"house black big."

00:29:31.340 --> 00:29:37.640 align:middle line:84%
You say, "A little green
Japanese carving knife," right?

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:42.530 align:middle line:84%
And in these languages, you
would say "A knife little green

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:43.370 align:middle line:90%
Japanese carving."

00:29:43.370 --> 00:29:45.620 align:middle line:84%
So you have the adjectives
in the same order they'd be

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:47.060 align:middle line:90%
in English, but after the noun.

00:29:47.060 --> 00:29:49.050 align:middle line:84%
That's one kind of
adjective-after-the-noun

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:49.550 align:middle line:90%
language.

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:50.967 align:middle line:84%
And the other kind
is just the one

00:29:50.967 --> 00:29:53.730 align:middle line:84%
that has the mirror
image of the adjectives.

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:55.940 align:middle line:84%
So in the languages of the
world, there are, I think,

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:58.280 align:middle line:84%
three kinds, which is
kind of interesting.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:29:59.210 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:00.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why is
it sort of right

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:03.560 align:middle line:84%
to say "big black house"
but not "black big house?"

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.120 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Hey,
good question, yes.

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:07.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:11.310 align:middle line:84%
There are linguists
working on that, yes.

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:13.990 align:middle line:84%
And that's actually basically
her question, your question,

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:15.240 align:middle line:90%
from just a second ago, right?

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:19.620 align:middle line:84%
So there are conditions on the
ordering of the adjectives.

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:22.380 align:middle line:84%
And yes, we want to try to
understand what those are.

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:24.120 align:middle line:84%
All of you are
eventually going to want

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:26.035 align:middle line:84%
to write a research
proposal in which you

00:30:26.035 --> 00:30:28.410 align:middle line:84%
try to tell me that you're
going to figure something out.

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:29.940 align:middle line:84%
I can give you
some things to read

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:31.470 align:middle line:84%
that are attempting
to figure out

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:32.760 align:middle line:90%
the answer to that question.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:37.000 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, that's
a research topic.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:37.960 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:41.370 align:middle line:90%
Cool, all right.

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:45.600 align:middle line:90%


00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:49.470 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, this is just me
saying again what I said.

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:51.330 align:middle line:84%
So what those trees
have in common

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.640 align:middle line:84%
is that if A is higher
than B in one tree,

00:30:53.640 --> 00:30:55.440 align:middle line:84%
the same A is higher
than the same B

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:58.350 align:middle line:84%
in the corresponding
tree, where by

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:01.680 align:middle line:84%
"higher," we mean
higher in the tree,

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:05.310 align:middle line:84%
added later in the series
of merge operations.

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:06.210 align:middle line:90%
That's what we mean.

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.820 align:middle line:90%


00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:11.580 align:middle line:84%
Lots of questions, like the
question that both of you

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:12.300 align:middle line:90%
asked--

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:14.610 align:middle line:84%
what's going on with
order of adjectives,

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:16.740 align:middle line:84%
or how come these
things have to be

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:19.290 align:middle line:84%
merged in these orders in all
of the trees that I'm showing.

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:21.832 align:middle line:84%
And I'm not offering to answer
those questions for you today.

00:31:21.832 --> 00:31:24.330 align:middle line:84%
These are some of the
central questions of syntax,

00:31:24.330 --> 00:31:27.363 align:middle line:84%
and we'll try to
understand them eventually.

00:31:27.363 --> 00:31:31.400 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah?

00:31:31.400 --> 00:31:34.070 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we started
off by saying there

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:37.498 align:middle line:84%
are at least two imaginable
kinds of lexicons if we're

00:31:37.498 --> 00:31:39.290 align:middle line:84%
going to make a list
of everything you know

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:44.720 align:middle line:84%
about your language, one that
has in it entries like "teach,"

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:47.420 align:middle line:84%
"teacher," "teachers,"
"teaching," and "mine,"

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:50.040 align:middle line:84%
"miner," "miners,"
"mining," and so on,

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:52.910 align:middle line:84%
and another one
that instead has--

00:31:52.910 --> 00:31:57.330 align:middle line:84%
so that first lexicon
consists entirely of words.

00:31:57.330 --> 00:32:00.290 align:middle line:84%
If you look at an actual
dictionary, that's

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:02.450 align:middle line:84%
the kind of lexicon you're
probably used to seeing.

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:03.950 align:middle line:84%
But we've been
fooling with the idea

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:06.950 align:middle line:84%
that your mental lexicon
is not organized that way.

00:32:06.950 --> 00:32:09.300 align:middle line:84%
What your mental lexicon
has in it is morphemes.

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:12.830 align:middle line:84%
So it has "teach," and "mine,"
and suffixes like "-er"

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:17.210 align:middle line:84%
and "-ing," and rules for how
morphemes can be combined with

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:18.950 align:middle line:84%
each other, and
what the result is,

00:32:18.950 --> 00:32:21.750 align:middle line:84%
and what happens when morphemes
combine with each other.

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:24.320 align:middle line:84%
That's the kind of model of
how language is put together

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:25.880 align:middle line:84%
that we've been
playing around with.

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:27.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:28.940 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:30.410 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:32:30.410 --> 00:32:33.620 align:middle line:84%
Yes, so just to
give these theories

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:35.960 align:middle line:84%
names, nice nonjudgmental
names, we're

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:39.350 align:middle line:84%
going to call them the wrong
theory and the right theory.

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:43.910 align:middle line:84%
And evidence that the
right theory is right,

00:32:43.910 --> 00:32:47.210 align:middle line:90%
that we played around with--

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:49.160 align:middle line:84%
for one thing, I
asked you to take pity

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:50.600 align:middle line:90%
on the plight of the Nimborans.

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:53.240 align:middle line:84%
These are these people in Papua
New Guinea whose verbs each

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:55.970 align:middle line:90%
have 27,000 forms.

00:32:55.970 --> 00:32:59.360 align:middle line:84%
If we make their lexicons
have every verb form in it,

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:01.250 align:middle line:84%
their lexicons are
going to be very large.

00:33:01.250 --> 00:33:04.040 align:middle line:84%
They will have no time to do
anything other than sit around

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:07.340 align:middle line:84%
and remember verbs,
whereas if we're

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:11.300 align:middle line:84%
willing to allow them to
break words into morphemes

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:13.070 align:middle line:84%
and put those in
their lexicons, then

00:33:13.070 --> 00:33:15.860 align:middle line:84%
they will have lexicons
of a reasonable size.

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:19.490 align:middle line:84%
And we saw lots of evidence that
people manipulate morphemes,

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:24.270 align:middle line:84%
including the fact that you can
apply morphemes to new words.

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:27.848 align:middle line:84%
So if I tell you I've invented a
new thing, it's called a "wug,"

00:33:27.848 --> 00:33:29.390 align:middle line:84%
and then I show you
another one, then

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:31.740 align:middle line:84%
you will call the
two things "wugs."

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.927 align:middle line:84%
This is a classic experiment
by Jean Berko Gleason, which

00:33:34.927 --> 00:33:37.010 align:middle line:84%
she did this with small
children and they did what

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:39.030 align:middle line:90%
you would expect them to do--

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:40.610 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:41.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:41.690 align:middle line:90%
Good?

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:44.060 align:middle line:84%
People are willing to
believe in morphemes?

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:45.990 align:middle line:90%
Excellent.

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:47.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:52.380 align:middle line:90%
I want to spend some time, then.

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:55.650 align:middle line:84%
The problem set that is now
finally available to you

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:58.390 align:middle line:90%
has on it a morphology problem.

00:33:58.390 --> 00:34:01.290 align:middle line:84%
So it gives you a bunch
of data from a language.

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:02.940 align:middle line:84%
The language is
Inupiaq, which is

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:07.620 align:middle line:84%
a language spoken in
the Arctic, in Alaska,

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:10.659 align:middle line:90%
in the coast of Alaska.

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:12.210 align:middle line:84%
And it asks you to
go through and try

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:13.877 align:middle line:84%
to find all the
morphemes and figure out

00:34:13.877 --> 00:34:15.852 align:middle line:90%
rules for how they combine.

00:34:15.852 --> 00:34:18.060 align:middle line:84%
We've already done a little
bit of that kind of thing

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:22.020 align:middle line:84%
in this class, but I wanted to
do another problem like this.

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:25.355 align:middle line:84%
And this problem is going
to introduce some new issues

00:34:25.355 --> 00:34:26.730 align:middle line:84%
and things for
you to think about

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:27.855 align:middle line:90%
as you're doing morphology.

00:34:27.855 --> 00:34:29.585 align:middle line:90%
So I want to go through these.

00:34:29.585 --> 00:34:31.710 align:middle line:84%
These data are from a
language called Lardil, which

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:32.960 align:middle line:90%
I think I've mentioned before.

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:35.050 align:middle line:84%
It's a language spoken
in northern Australia

00:34:35.050 --> 00:34:38.550 align:middle line:84%
which I've done
in field work on.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:42.750 align:middle line:84%
It's a language that has
inflection on its nouns.

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:44.710 align:middle line:84%
Its nouns get
inflected for case.

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:47.830 align:middle line:84%
So they have suffixes
indicating case.

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:51.210 align:middle line:84%
And if you're not sure what
I meant by that, here's

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:52.560 align:middle line:90%
what I mean.

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:55.080 align:middle line:84%
Nouns have little markers
on them telling basically

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:57.940 align:middle line:84%
whether they are the subject
or the object of a verb.

00:34:57.940 --> 00:34:59.610 align:middle line:84%
So if you're the
subject of a verb,

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:01.270 align:middle line:90%
you're in the nominative case.

00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:05.680 align:middle line:84%
And if you're the object, then
you're in the accusative case.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:08.010 align:middle line:84%
If you study-- English does
not have so much of this,

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.180 align:middle line:90%
except on our pronouns.

00:35:09.180 --> 00:35:10.770 align:middle line:84%
If you study lots
of other languages,

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:15.037 align:middle line:84%
if you try to learn Russian,
or Japanese, or Latin,

00:35:15.037 --> 00:35:17.370 align:middle line:84%
or many other languages,
you've had to learn about case.

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:18.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:21.210 align:middle line:90%
So Lardil has case.

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:24.810 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of nouns in
their nominative and accusative

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:25.620 align:middle line:90%
forms.

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So you've got "mela,"
which means "seawater."

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:30.690 align:middle line:84%
It also means "beer,"
I guess on the theory

00:35:30.690 --> 00:35:33.510 align:middle line:84%
that they're both foamy
things that make you sick

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:35.790 align:middle line:90%
if you drink too much of it.

00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:39.810 align:middle line:84%
"Mela," "barnga," "thungal,"
"ketharr," and "miyar," those

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:42.030 align:middle line:84%
are the Lardil nouns in
their nominative forms.

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.072 align:middle line:84%
And then you've got their
accusative forms there,

00:35:44.072 --> 00:35:46.080 align:middle line:90%
which I won't read to you.

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:48.944 align:middle line:90%
What's the accusative morpheme?

00:35:48.944 --> 00:35:50.360 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "-n."

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:51.530 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "-n."

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:52.550 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:54.347 align:middle line:90%
"-n," yeah.

00:35:54.347 --> 00:35:56.930 align:middle line:84%
Oh dear, I'm going to have to
do this writing-everything-twice

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:57.430 align:middle line:90%
thing again.

00:35:57.430 --> 00:36:01.050 align:middle line:84%
I really need to learn how to
manipulate the screens better.

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:04.760 align:middle line:84%
So the accusative
suffix is "-n."

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:07.020 align:middle line:84%
Say it again, the
accusative suffix is "-n."

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:11.820 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody find any examples
that are a problem for that?

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:12.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:14.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The ones
that have "-in."

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:16.835 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so some
of them have "-in," right?

00:36:16.835 --> 00:36:19.830 align:middle line:84%
Like "tree," and "river,"
and "spear," I think.

00:36:19.830 --> 00:36:23.190 align:middle line:84%
What determines whether the
suffix is "-n" or "-in"?

00:36:23.190 --> 00:36:24.237 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:36:24.237 --> 00:36:25.320 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The last letter.

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:29.355 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The last
letter of the nominative, yep.

00:36:29.355 --> 00:36:30.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "a" or not "a."

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:32.220 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Can
you be more specific?

00:36:32.220 --> 00:36:35.460 align:middle line:84%
What determines whether
it's just an N or an I-N?

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.633 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If the noun ends in
"a," then it ends with "-n."

00:36:38.633 --> 00:36:40.312 align:middle line:84%
If it ends in not
"a," then it's "-in."

00:36:40.312 --> 00:36:41.520 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That's true.

00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:44.690 align:middle line:84%
All of these examples,
it ends in an "a."

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:46.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are they
vowel versus consonant?

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:48.555 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
so we might bravely

00:36:48.555 --> 00:36:50.430 align:middle line:84%
say that it's about
vowels versus consonants,

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:51.060 align:middle line:90%
and we'd be right.

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:52.227 align:middle line:90%
But you're absolutely right.

00:36:52.227 --> 00:36:54.120 align:middle line:90%
I haven't shown you that.

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:56.910 align:middle line:84%
So the fact is,
after a consonant,

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:59.310 align:middle line:84%
you get "-in," and after
a vowel, you get "n."

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:02.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say
that again over here.

00:37:02.250 --> 00:37:05.310 align:middle line:90%
After a consonant, you get--

00:37:05.310 --> 00:37:07.410 align:middle line:84%
sorry, after a
vowel, you get "-n."

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:09.870 align:middle line:84%
And after a consonant,
you get -in."

00:37:09.870 --> 00:37:11.110 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:37:11.110 --> 00:37:11.610 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:12.450 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:15.200 align:middle line:90%


00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:16.370 align:middle line:90%
Here are some more nouns.

00:37:16.370 --> 00:37:19.350 align:middle line:84%
So the nouns appear, the
nouns you saw before.

00:37:19.350 --> 00:37:20.840 align:middle line:90%
There are some more nouns.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:24.830 align:middle line:84%
"Rain" is when "wunda,"
"tip" is "belda," and "curve"

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:26.150 align:middle line:90%
is "dalda."

00:37:26.150 --> 00:37:30.080 align:middle line:84%
And the accusatives are
"wunin," "belin," and "dalin."

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:34.325 align:middle line:84%
That's unexpected, right,
given our theory so far?

00:37:34.325 --> 00:37:39.900 align:middle line:90%


00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:42.140 align:middle line:90%
What do you think is going on?

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:42.770 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:46.807 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you remove the
"-da" at the end, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:46.807 --> 00:37:48.890 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So it's
as though you're removing

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:52.550 align:middle line:84%
the "-da," right,
at the end, yeah?

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:53.501 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:37:53.501 --> 00:37:56.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What you think is--

00:37:56.150 --> 00:38:01.100 align:middle line:84%
I think it's more than just
"-da," because you have "wunda"

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:02.030 align:middle line:90%
opposite to "katha."

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:03.238 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, we do.

00:38:03.238 --> 00:38:04.310 align:middle line:90%
We have a minimal pair.

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:06.210 align:middle line:84%
It was sneaky of me
to put that in there.

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:10.350 align:middle line:84%
So there's a "wunda" that
means a type of stingray.

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:11.990 align:middle line:84%
The accusative of
that is "wundan."

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:14.397 align:middle line:84%
And then there's another one
down here that means rain,

00:38:14.397 --> 00:38:15.980 align:middle line:84%
and the accusative
of that is "wunin."

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:18.580 align:middle line:90%


00:38:18.580 --> 00:38:22.210 align:middle line:84%
So here I am asking you, how
do we get from the nominative

00:38:22.210 --> 00:38:23.800 align:middle line:90%
to the accusative?

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:24.607 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:24.607 --> 00:38:26.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is "-da" a morpheme
that turns everything else

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:28.040 align:middle line:90%
into the nominative?

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:29.260 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It
would be nice, but no.

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:31.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, actually that's an
interesting way to say it.

00:38:31.510 --> 00:38:34.160 align:middle line:84%
There's a way to say what you
just said that would be true.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:35.800 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I shouldn't
just say no.

00:38:35.800 --> 00:38:36.550 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:38:36.550 --> 00:38:40.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is the verb actually
something else, and they're

00:38:40.060 --> 00:38:43.660 align:middle line:84%
both being modified to form
the causative adjective?

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:46.450 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I think you
and you-- actually, I'm sorry.

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:48.580 align:middle line:84%
I shouldn't have
spoken so quickly--

00:38:48.580 --> 00:38:49.660 align:middle line:90%
are on the right track.

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:51.580 align:middle line:84%
Look, here I am
saying, how can we

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:53.890 align:middle line:84%
get from the nominative
to the accusative, right?

00:38:53.890 --> 00:38:56.620 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is
despair, right?

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:59.320 align:middle line:84%
That's what that minimal
pair is meant to do.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:01.090 align:middle line:84%
We had the same thing
in Polish, right?

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:04.600 align:middle line:84%
Where the word for "lye" and
the word for something else

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:07.948 align:middle line:84%
were the same in the singular
but different in the plural.

00:39:07.948 --> 00:39:09.490 align:middle line:84%
So we convinced
ourselves, all right,

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:12.280 align:middle line:84%
we can't predict the
plural from the singular.

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:14.320 align:middle line:84%
But we can predict the
singular from the plural.

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:15.710 align:middle line:90%
That's what we decided, right?

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:18.760 align:middle line:84%
So in Polish, we said some
of these nouns underlyingly

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:20.980 align:middle line:84%
end in "g," some of them
underlyingly end in "k."

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:23.320 align:middle line:84%
And there's a general
rule that "g" becomes

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:24.705 align:middle line:90%
"k" at the end of a word.

00:39:24.705 --> 00:39:26.080 align:middle line:84%
Can we do something
similar here?

00:39:26.080 --> 00:39:27.650 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:29.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Also, how do
we know that "wunda" is

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:32.002 align:middle line:90%
"wunda," the stingray species?

00:39:32.002 --> 00:39:35.035 align:middle line:84%
Is there some kind of
exception to the rule?

00:39:35.035 --> 00:39:38.530 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Could
be that, could be that.

00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:40.570 align:middle line:90%
Two things, it isn't.

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:43.750 align:middle line:90%
It's part of a general thing.

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:45.670 align:middle line:90%
But also, wait.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:46.840 align:middle line:90%
You're absolutely right.

00:39:46.840 --> 00:39:48.880 align:middle line:84%
Whenever we're
looking at a data set,

00:39:48.880 --> 00:39:50.380 align:middle line:84%
we could say to
ourselves, well, you

00:39:50.380 --> 00:39:52.060 align:middle line:84%
know what, maybe this
is just an exception.

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:53.870 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I shouldn't try
to account for this.

00:39:53.870 --> 00:39:59.280 align:middle line:84%
But in this class, you should
trust me not to do that to you.

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:01.460 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to give you
data sets in which you

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:04.190 align:middle line:84%
will be able to come up with
rules that account for things.

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:08.930 align:middle line:84%
And in life, that shouldn't
be your first move, probably,

00:40:08.930 --> 00:40:09.863 align:middle line:90%
because--

00:40:09.863 --> 00:40:11.030 align:middle line:90%
I don't want to pick on you.

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:13.310 align:middle line:84%
It's a move you hear
people make a lot.

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:15.110 align:middle line:90%
Hey, maybe this is an exception.

00:40:15.110 --> 00:40:17.240 align:middle line:84%
But making that
your first move is

00:40:17.240 --> 00:40:19.250 align:middle line:84%
kind of a recipe
for failing to make

00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:20.465 align:middle line:90%
exciting discoveries, right?

00:40:20.465 --> 00:40:22.340 align:middle line:84%
So if you're willing to
exclude certain data,

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:23.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm just not going
to account for that.

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:25.520 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, that's the
way to actually account

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:27.520 align:middle line:84%
for everything, is by
being willing to recognize

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:28.310 align:middle line:90%
exceptions.

00:40:28.310 --> 00:40:30.770 align:middle line:84%
But other times, what it does
is stop you from realizing,

00:40:30.770 --> 00:40:33.530 align:middle line:84%
no, the exceptions have
a general character.

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:36.050 align:middle line:84%
We can understand
what they all are.

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:37.820 align:middle line:90%
So it's worth like--

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:39.560 align:middle line:84%
maybe that can be
part of a strategy,

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:41.420 align:middle line:84%
corralling all the
exceptions into one place

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:43.045 align:middle line:84%
and then looking at
them all and trying

00:40:43.045 --> 00:40:45.302 align:middle line:90%
to understand what's going on.

00:40:45.302 --> 00:40:46.760 align:middle line:84%
In this particular
case, the things

00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:49.460 align:middle line:84%
that look exceptional right now
are "rain," "tip," and "curve."

00:40:49.460 --> 00:40:51.860 align:middle line:84%
We had this beautiful rule
on the basis of the words

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:52.910 align:middle line:90%
that were up at the top.

00:40:52.910 --> 00:40:55.490 align:middle line:84%
And now, these new data
are breaking it, right?

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:58.820 align:middle line:84%
We want the accusative of
"belda" to be "beldan."

00:40:58.820 --> 00:41:02.930 align:middle line:84%
The fact that it's
"belin" is weird.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:04.910 align:middle line:84%
Is there a way to
do the trick that we

00:41:04.910 --> 00:41:09.890 align:middle line:84%
did for Polish, which was to
say the plural is the useful--

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:12.765 align:middle line:84%
is the thing that we can use
to predict the singular, right?

00:41:12.765 --> 00:41:14.390 align:middle line:84%
So we'll start with
the plural and then

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:16.140 align:middle line:84%
we'll figure out how
to make the singular.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:20.000 align:middle line:90%
Can we play that game here?

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:20.893 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:20.893 --> 00:41:22.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
a case form now

00:41:22.310 --> 00:41:25.370 align:middle line:84%
that we don't know, that is, you
get something added onto that

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:27.410 align:middle line:84%
complement to nominative
to form the accusative?

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:29.960 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So maybe
that's a way to think about it.

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:32.335 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is more or
less what several people have

00:41:32.335 --> 00:41:32.930 align:middle line:90%
been saying.

00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:39.590 align:middle line:84%
What do you wish the nominative
of this was, given our rules?

00:41:39.590 --> 00:41:44.330 align:middle line:84%
So we wish that they were
"wun," "bel," and "dal," right?

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:50.940 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so I'm just going to
write that down, "wun," "bel,"

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:52.540 align:middle line:90%
and "dal."

00:41:52.540 --> 00:41:54.600 align:middle line:84%
And then if it
were that, well, we

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:56.795 align:middle line:84%
would be able to
get the accusative,

00:41:56.795 --> 00:41:58.170 align:middle line:84%
and we would need
to do something

00:41:58.170 --> 00:41:59.760 align:middle line:90%
to get the nominative.

00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:00.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Vlada?

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:02.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I want to say
something about the fact

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:04.490 align:middle line:84%
that all of them
are one syllable.

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:06.948 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] for whatever reason.

00:42:06.948 --> 00:42:09.240 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think that
would be very attractive,

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:11.435 align:middle line:84%
so I think that is
raising a good point.

00:42:11.435 --> 00:42:13.560 align:middle line:84%
All of the other words that
I've shown you, "mela,"

00:42:13.560 --> 00:42:15.977 align:middle line:84%
"barnga," "katha," "wunda,"
"thungal," "ketharr," "miyar,"

00:42:15.977 --> 00:42:18.390 align:middle line:90%
they're all two syllables long.

00:42:18.390 --> 00:42:22.140 align:middle line:84%
These words, if there were words
like "wun," "bel," and "dal,"

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:25.410 align:middle line:84%
they would be our first
Lardil monosyllables.

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:27.270 align:middle line:90%
Now, here's a fact about Lardil.

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:30.180 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't have monosyllables.

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:35.360 align:middle line:84%
All of its words are at
least two syllables long.

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:37.590 align:middle line:84%
So apparently, I think
Vlada is absolutely right.

00:42:37.590 --> 00:42:40.220 align:middle line:84%
Apparently that's
being enforced here.

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:42.563 align:middle line:90%
So indeed, these words--

00:42:42.563 --> 00:42:44.480 align:middle line:84%
a whole bunch of people
said versions of this.

00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:46.670 align:middle line:84%
Let me just channel what
you all said-- these words

00:42:46.670 --> 00:42:48.800 align:middle line:84%
at the end, "rain,"
"tip," and "curve," they

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:53.210 align:middle line:84%
have base forms that don't
appear anywhere, in a sense.

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:55.560 align:middle line:84%
Their base forms are
"wun," "bel," and "dal,"

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:57.540 align:middle line:84%
and that's what you
add the accusative to,

00:42:57.540 --> 00:42:59.180 align:middle line:90%
by a totally regular rule.

00:42:59.180 --> 00:43:03.170 align:middle line:84%
They end in consonants, and
so the accusative is in.

00:43:03.170 --> 00:43:05.030 align:middle line:84%
And then we need
another rule that

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:14.760 align:middle line:84%
says, "Add -da to
monosyllables,"

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:16.290 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:21.180 align:middle line:84%
So if you're in danger of having
a monosyllabic Lardil word,

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:26.580 align:middle line:90%
you add -da to monosyllables.

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:30.900 align:middle line:84%
Lardil doesn't like words that
are only one syllable long.

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:33.030 align:middle line:84%
This is what's called a
minimal word requirement.

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:34.990 align:middle line:84%
They're cross-linguistically
quite common.

00:43:34.990 --> 00:43:37.365 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of languages
in the world that don't like

00:43:37.365 --> 00:43:38.926 align:middle line:90%
their words to be too short.

00:43:38.926 --> 00:43:40.640 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:42.560 align:middle line:90%
All right, good.

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:43.700 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good.

00:43:43.700 --> 00:43:46.820 align:middle line:90%


00:43:46.820 --> 00:43:49.560 align:middle line:84%
Right, so what we want to do
is posit underlying forms,

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:52.490 align:middle line:84%
which are often the same as
the nominative, but not always.

00:43:52.490 --> 00:43:56.090 align:middle line:84%
There also these last three
that have different forms, OK?

00:43:56.090 --> 00:43:56.820 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:59.360 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Then, just to
clarify, "wun," "dal,"

00:43:59.360 --> 00:44:02.240 align:middle line:90%
and "wunda" are all morphemes?

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:03.470 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well, OK.

00:44:03.470 --> 00:44:06.440 align:middle line:90%
"Wun" is a morpheme.

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:11.170 align:middle line:84%
"Wunda" up there, stingray
species, is a morpheme.

00:44:11.170 --> 00:44:13.660 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if we want to
call this a morpheme or not.

00:44:13.660 --> 00:44:16.480 align:middle line:84%
This is why I was reacting
the way I was before.

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:19.090 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't mean anything, right?

00:44:19.090 --> 00:44:23.320 align:middle line:84%
It's just there so the
word can be long enough.

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:25.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's all it's for.

00:44:25.150 --> 00:44:29.740 align:middle line:84%
So I think linguists would
mostly not call it a morpheme.

00:44:29.740 --> 00:44:34.570 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of like
we said for Polish,

00:44:34.570 --> 00:44:38.140 align:middle line:84%
if a word ends with a "g,"
the "g' becomes a "k."

00:44:38.140 --> 00:44:41.230 align:middle line:84%
The change from a "g" to
a "k" isn't a morpheme.

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:44.750 align:middle line:84%
It's part of something that
Polish prefers about its words.

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:46.450 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't like them
to end with "k."

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:48.730 align:middle line:84%
Lardil doesn't like words
to be monosyllables,

00:44:48.730 --> 00:44:50.200 align:middle line:90%
and this is its repair.

00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:53.570 align:middle line:90%
It's how it fixes that problem.

00:44:53.570 --> 00:44:54.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:44:54.850 --> 00:44:57.410 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm confused a little.

00:44:57.410 --> 00:44:58.870 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
I don't blame you.

00:44:58.870 --> 00:45:03.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They don't want
monosyllables as words,

00:45:03.190 --> 00:45:07.990 align:middle line:84%
but they create
monosyllable morphemes

00:45:07.990 --> 00:45:13.005 align:middle line:84%
and build whole verbs on
those two [INAUDIBLE]..

00:45:13.005 --> 00:45:15.130 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, I
totally see what you mean.

00:45:15.130 --> 00:45:16.990 align:middle line:84%
So let me just repeat
what you just said.

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:19.660 align:middle line:84%
These people don't like
monosyllables, right?

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:24.910 align:middle line:84%
And yet their lexicon is
full of monosyllables, right?

00:45:24.910 --> 00:45:27.250 align:middle line:84%
It's as though, I don't
know, somebody else

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:29.325 align:middle line:90%
gave them their lexicon, right?

00:45:29.325 --> 00:45:30.700 align:middle line:84%
And then they're
doing their best

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:33.310 align:middle line:84%
to improve it in
the ways that will

00:45:33.310 --> 00:45:34.810 align:middle line:90%
make them comfortable with it.

00:45:34.810 --> 00:45:37.870 align:middle line:84%
Or another way to
say it may be when

00:45:37.870 --> 00:45:40.090 align:middle line:84%
we say they don't
like monosyllables,

00:45:40.090 --> 00:45:44.050 align:middle line:84%
they don't like
pronouncing monosyllables.

00:45:44.050 --> 00:45:46.845 align:middle line:84%
They don't mind monosyllables
in their lexicon,

00:45:46.845 --> 00:45:48.970 align:middle line:84%
but they're not willing to
pronounce them that way.

00:45:48.970 --> 00:45:51.940 align:middle line:84%
They have to do something
to them, kind of like Poles

00:45:51.940 --> 00:45:54.220 align:middle line:84%
don't mind words that end
with G as long as they're

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:55.750 align:middle line:90%
in their brains, right?

00:45:55.750 --> 00:45:56.725 align:middle line:90%
It's just saying them.

00:45:56.725 --> 00:45:57.850 align:middle line:90%
They don't like doing that.

00:45:57.850 --> 00:46:01.570 align:middle line:84%
They have to do
something to them first.

00:46:01.570 --> 00:46:02.680 align:middle line:90%
Nice point, actually.

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:05.560 align:middle line:84%
This is one of many
places in linguistics

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:08.140 align:middle line:84%
where you get the feeling
that languages are designed

00:46:08.140 --> 00:46:11.533 align:middle line:84%
by committees which have
different priorities,

00:46:11.533 --> 00:46:14.200 align:middle line:84%
that there's one group of people
who are like making the lexicon

00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:16.780 align:middle line:84%
and another group of
people who are like--

00:46:16.780 --> 00:46:19.202 align:middle line:84%
I imagine them taking reports
from the first committee.

00:46:19.202 --> 00:46:20.410 align:middle line:90%
And then they're like, "What?

00:46:20.410 --> 00:46:21.220 align:middle line:90%
You gave us what?

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Look at all these
monosyllables."

00:46:22.810 --> 00:46:25.680 align:middle line:90%


00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:26.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:28.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:32.260 align:middle line:84%
So going further
into Lardil then.

00:46:32.260 --> 00:46:34.390 align:middle line:90%
Here are some more words.

00:46:34.390 --> 00:46:36.220 align:middle line:84%
The word for "fish"
is "yaka," the word

00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:39.850 align:middle line:84%
for "string" is "birrka," and
the word for "head" is "lelka."

00:46:39.850 --> 00:46:45.250 align:middle line:84%
And the accusatives are "yakin,"
"birrkin," and "lelkin."

00:46:45.250 --> 00:46:48.090 align:middle line:90%


00:46:48.090 --> 00:46:49.860 align:middle line:84%
Is everyone appropriately
upset by this?

00:46:49.860 --> 00:46:54.930 align:middle line:84%
What would we have expected
the accusative of "yaka" to be,

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:56.320 align:middle line:90%
the word for fish?

00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:57.070 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Yakan?"

00:46:57.070 --> 00:46:58.362 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Yakan," yeah.

00:46:58.362 --> 00:47:01.040 align:middle line:90%


00:47:01.040 --> 00:47:03.200 align:middle line:90%
What should we do?

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:03.897 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:47:03.897 --> 00:47:05.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
a set of consonants

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:07.355 align:middle line:84%
that you're not allowed
to end the word with?

00:47:07.355 --> 00:47:11.680 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah,
so why do you ask?

00:47:11.680 --> 00:47:13.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, they
all end with an "a."

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:15.597 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I
mean, they don't, right?

00:47:15.597 --> 00:47:19.370 align:middle line:84%
"Yaka" ends in a vowel and
"yakin" ends in an "n," right?

00:47:19.370 --> 00:47:21.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:47:21.050 --> 00:47:22.985 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Supposing
that they have--

00:47:22.985 --> 00:47:25.820 align:middle line:84%
that the underlying
form of "yaka" is "yak,"

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:28.970 align:middle line:84%
but you can't end it with
"k," so you have to put--

00:47:28.970 --> 00:47:30.860 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
these three, if we

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:32.855 align:middle line:90%
went from the accusative--

00:47:32.855 --> 00:47:33.730 align:middle line:90%
what are these three?

00:47:33.730 --> 00:47:35.660 align:middle line:90%
"Yaka," "birrka," and "lelka."

00:47:35.660 --> 00:47:38.395 align:middle line:84%
If we went from the
accusative, if we started--

00:47:38.395 --> 00:47:40.520 align:middle line:84%
if we did the same trick
we did with the last three

00:47:40.520 --> 00:47:43.272 align:middle line:84%
and said, OK, the nominative,
I don't understand why

00:47:43.272 --> 00:47:44.480 align:middle line:90%
the nominative is what it is.

00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:46.460 align:middle line:84%
But looking just
at the accusative,

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:51.830 align:middle line:84%
I wish that the underlying form
of "fish" were "yak," right?

00:47:51.830 --> 00:47:52.880 align:middle line:90%
That would work.

00:47:52.880 --> 00:47:55.490 align:middle line:84%
So our rule for
accusatives would get

00:47:55.490 --> 00:47:56.780 align:middle line:90%
you the accusatives of "yak."

00:47:56.780 --> 00:47:59.230 align:middle line:84%
It would be "yakin," and
everything would be perfect.

00:47:59.230 --> 00:48:03.230 align:middle line:84%
And then the question is, why
isn't the nominative "yak,"

00:48:03.230 --> 00:48:05.300 align:middle line:84%
to which the answer
could be Vlada's answer,

00:48:05.300 --> 00:48:07.580 align:middle line:90%
it's only one syllable long.

00:48:07.580 --> 00:48:13.400 align:middle line:84%
But then why isn't the
nominative of "yak" "yakda?"

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:15.770 align:middle line:84%
And Joseph had a
suggestion about that.

00:48:15.770 --> 00:48:20.630 align:middle line:84%
"Yak," "birrk," and
"lelk" all end in a "k."

00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:23.210 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I've got two
general rule here.

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:25.070 align:middle line:84%
It's not add -da
to monosyllables.

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:31.700 align:middle line:84%
It's add "-da" to, I'll
say, most monosyllables.

00:48:31.700 --> 00:48:37.580 align:middle line:90%
Add "-a" after "k."

00:48:37.580 --> 00:48:41.120 align:middle line:84%
I'll say the same
thing over here.

00:48:41.120 --> 00:48:44.330 align:middle line:90%
Add -da to most monosyllables.

00:48:44.330 --> 00:48:49.630 align:middle line:90%
Add "-a" after "k."

00:48:49.630 --> 00:48:50.130 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:50.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:48:50.700 --> 00:48:52.617 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you not
have two stops in a row?

00:48:52.617 --> 00:48:54.943 align:middle line:90%
Variable.

00:48:54.943 --> 00:48:57.110 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You cannot
have two stops in a row.

00:48:57.110 --> 00:48:58.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You have
to have a vowel?

00:48:58.190 --> 00:48:59.240 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yup,
that would break that.

00:48:59.240 --> 00:49:00.180 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:49:00.180 --> 00:49:04.490 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any Lardil words
that have two stops in a row.

00:49:04.490 --> 00:49:06.515 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's true.

00:49:06.515 --> 00:49:07.015 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:49:07.015 --> 00:49:09.980 align:middle line:90%


00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:14.510 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, "yak,"
"birrk," and "lelk," OK?

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:16.370 align:middle line:84%
I think this is the
last set of Lardil data

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:19.460 align:middle line:84%
I was going to show you,
if I'm remembering right.

00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:20.300 align:middle line:90%
No, it's not.

00:49:20.300 --> 00:49:22.530 align:middle line:90%
There are some others, sorry.

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:26.480 align:middle line:84%
"Kanda" is blood, "nguka"
is water, "ngawa" is dog,

00:49:26.480 --> 00:49:29.300 align:middle line:90%
and "karda" is a kinship term.

00:49:29.300 --> 00:49:33.920 align:middle line:84%
Lardil kinship terminology is
extremely different from ours

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:36.200 align:middle line:84%
in ways that would take
a long time to explain.

00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:38.870 align:middle line:84%
But "karda" roughly
means a woman's child

00:49:38.870 --> 00:49:40.170 align:middle line:90%
or a man's sister's child.

00:49:40.170 --> 00:49:43.330 align:middle line:90%


00:49:43.330 --> 00:49:46.390 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, no, I won't try
to explain that to you.

00:49:46.390 --> 00:49:49.150 align:middle line:84%
It's very interesting,
but I refuse

00:49:49.150 --> 00:49:51.760 align:middle line:90%
to talk about it right now.

00:49:51.760 --> 00:49:56.180 align:middle line:84%
So the accusative of
"kanda" is "kandun,"

00:49:56.180 --> 00:49:57.590 align:middle line:90%
which it shouldn't be, right?

00:49:57.590 --> 00:50:01.700 align:middle line:84%
It should be "kandan," if it
were going to be anything.

00:50:01.700 --> 00:50:02.900 align:middle line:90%
Do we want the underlying--

00:50:02.900 --> 00:50:04.963 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we have a
rule that adds "-da."

00:50:04.963 --> 00:50:06.755 align:middle line:84%
Do we want the underlying
form to be "kan?"

00:50:06.755 --> 00:50:12.700 align:middle line:90%


00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:14.170 align:middle line:90%
No, right?

00:50:14.170 --> 00:50:15.760 align:middle line:84%
If the underlying
form were "kan,"

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:18.050 align:middle line:90%
what would the accusative be?

00:50:18.050 --> 00:50:18.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Kanin."

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:20.835 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Kanin,"
yeah, and it's not.

00:50:20.835 --> 00:50:22.200 align:middle line:90%
It's "kandun."

00:50:22.200 --> 00:50:23.825 align:middle line:90%
So we need another rule.

00:50:23.825 --> 00:50:25.200 align:middle line:84%
What's the new
rule going to say?

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:29.280 align:middle line:90%


00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:30.300 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:31.650 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Just make a guess.

00:50:31.650 --> 00:50:34.110 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:50:34.110 --> 00:50:37.572 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can't
end it in "u," maybe.

00:50:37.572 --> 00:50:39.024 align:middle line:84%
So you'd have to
change everything

00:50:39.024 --> 00:50:43.965 align:middle line:84%
that ends in a "u' to an
"a" in the nominative.

00:50:43.965 --> 00:50:47.200 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:50.763 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Right, so we're going

00:50:50.763 --> 00:50:52.180 align:middle line:84%
to posit underlying
forms-- that's

00:50:52.180 --> 00:50:54.640 align:middle line:84%
nice-- we're going to posit
underlying forms like "kandu,"

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:56.620 align:middle line:90%
"nguku," "ngawu," "kardu."

00:50:56.620 --> 00:51:05.630 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to have a
rule, change final "u" to "a."

00:51:05.630 --> 00:51:09.070 align:middle line:84%
So "nguku," underlying
"nguku," gives you

00:51:09.070 --> 00:51:13.900 align:middle line:84%
the accusative "nguku," takes
the "n," and you get "ngukun."

00:51:13.900 --> 00:51:15.490 align:middle line:84%
But there's another
rule that says,

00:51:15.490 --> 00:51:22.130 align:middle line:84%
change final "u" to "a," that
in the nominative changes

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:23.260 align:middle line:90%
"nguku" to "nguka."

00:51:23.260 --> 00:51:24.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:51:24.190 --> 00:51:27.020 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:51:27.020 --> 00:51:29.980 align:middle line:90%
And then-- yup?

00:51:29.980 --> 00:51:30.480 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:51:30.480 --> 00:51:32.490 align:middle line:84%
And then I think
this is the last set

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:34.830 align:middle line:90%
of data I wanted to show you.

00:51:34.830 --> 00:51:36.660 align:middle line:84%
The word for "story"
is "ngalu," the word

00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:39.510 align:middle line:84%
for "boomerang" is "wangal,"
the word for "kookaburra"

00:51:39.510 --> 00:51:42.390 align:middle line:84%
is "thalkurr," and the word for
"umbilical cord" is "kundul,"

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:44.160 align:middle line:84%
but the accusatives
are "ngalukin,"

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:46.950 align:middle line:84%
"wangalkin," "thalkurrkin,"
and "kundulkin."

00:51:46.950 --> 00:51:50.240 align:middle line:84%
What's the underlying
form of these?

00:51:50.240 --> 00:51:51.980 align:middle line:84%
You guys all know
the drill by now.

00:51:51.980 --> 00:51:57.340 align:middle line:84%
It's like, take the accusative,
subtract the accusative suffix.

00:51:57.340 --> 00:52:02.050 align:middle line:84%
So what should the accusative
of "umbilical cord" be?

00:52:02.050 --> 00:52:07.290 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, what should the bare
form of "umbilical cord" be?

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:09.120 align:middle line:90%
"Kundulk," yeah?

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:10.020 align:middle line:90%
"Kundulk."

00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:12.600 align:middle line:84%
So we would expect "kundulk,"
"thalkurrk," "wangalk,"

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:14.310 align:middle line:90%
and "ngaluk."

00:52:14.310 --> 00:52:22.540 align:middle line:84%
And there's something
that says, drop final "k."

00:52:22.540 --> 00:52:27.310 align:middle line:84%
So "kundulk," we
start with "kundulk,"

00:52:27.310 --> 00:52:30.120 align:middle line:84%
and we form the
accusative off of that.

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:34.290 align:middle line:84%
But if it's nominative, we
get rid of the final "k."

00:52:34.290 --> 00:52:35.425 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:52:35.425 --> 00:52:37.612 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What
happened to "birrka?"

00:52:37.612 --> 00:52:39.070 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
What happens what?

00:52:39.070 --> 00:52:40.912 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What
happened to "birrka?"

00:52:40.912 --> 00:52:42.370 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
I missed it again.

00:52:42.370 --> 00:52:45.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why is
that not "birrka?"

00:52:45.250 --> 00:52:46.810 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Why is--

00:52:46.810 --> 00:52:48.190 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I think it's one way.

00:52:48.190 --> 00:52:50.800 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, the word
for string or whatever it was,

00:52:50.800 --> 00:52:54.340 align:middle line:84%
"birrka," which
started off as "birrk."

00:52:54.340 --> 00:52:56.600 align:middle line:84%
And we asked, why aren't
we dropping the final K?

00:52:56.600 --> 00:52:58.600 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you're asking exactly
the right question--

00:52:58.600 --> 00:53:00.910 align:middle line:90%
or similarly, "ngalu."

00:53:00.910 --> 00:53:02.680 align:middle line:84%
We just came up with
a rule that changes

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:04.810 align:middle line:90%
final "u" to "a," right?

00:53:04.810 --> 00:53:07.540 align:middle line:84%
But the word for
story is not "ngala."

00:53:07.540 --> 00:53:09.250 align:middle line:90%
It's "ngalu."

00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:10.062 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:53:10.062 --> 00:53:12.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do we have to specify
that dropping the final "k'

00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:14.685 align:middle line:84%
applies to underlying
forms internal to swap it?

00:53:14.685 --> 00:53:16.810 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So we
might have to say something.

00:53:16.810 --> 00:53:18.610 align:middle line:84%
We either have to
work on our rules--

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:20.102 align:middle line:84%
you're absolutely
right-- and make

00:53:20.102 --> 00:53:21.310 align:middle line:90%
them more specific than that.

00:53:21.310 --> 00:53:22.540 align:middle line:84%
That's one kind of
thing we could do.

00:53:22.540 --> 00:53:23.290 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:53:23.290 --> 00:53:24.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And I'm
wondering if there's

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:25.780 align:middle line:84%
some kind of like
declension system.

00:53:25.780 --> 00:53:27.863 align:middle line:84%
Like, I'm thinking if like
I had to study Lardil--

00:53:27.863 --> 00:53:29.155 align:middle line:90%
some kind of declension system.

00:53:29.155 --> 00:53:31.280 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So we
couldn't do that for Lardil.

00:53:31.280 --> 00:53:33.290 align:middle line:84%
We could say, you
know what, there

00:53:33.290 --> 00:53:35.990 align:middle line:84%
are nouns that do one
of these, and then

00:53:35.990 --> 00:53:38.310 align:middle line:84%
there are nouns that do
another set of these.

00:53:38.310 --> 00:53:40.970 align:middle line:84%
So there are rules that apply
to particular classes of nouns.

00:53:40.970 --> 00:53:44.430 align:middle line:84%
We could absolutely
say that, yeah.

00:53:44.430 --> 00:53:46.140 align:middle line:84%
Here's another thing
we could do, though.

00:53:46.140 --> 00:53:49.890 align:middle line:84%
So here's-- just to stick to
the case that you were worried

00:53:49.890 --> 00:53:52.713 align:middle line:90%
about just a second ago--

00:53:52.713 --> 00:53:53.880 align:middle line:90%
What did I do with my chalk?

00:53:53.880 --> 00:53:56.490 align:middle line:90%
I guess I took it over there.

00:53:56.490 --> 00:54:00.720 align:middle line:84%
We've got words like
"birrk," that means

00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:03.660 align:middle line:90%
string, underlying "birrk."

00:54:03.660 --> 00:54:06.110 align:middle line:84%
And then we've got words
like underlying "kundulk."

00:54:06.110 --> 00:54:09.280 align:middle line:90%


00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:12.010 align:middle line:84%
That means umbilical
cord, right?

00:54:12.010 --> 00:54:17.230 align:middle line:84%
And with "birrk," what we
do is add "a" because--

00:54:17.230 --> 00:54:18.790 align:middle line:90%
so what we do is add "a."

00:54:18.790 --> 00:54:23.530 align:middle line:84%
But with "kundulk," what we do
is get rid of the final "k."

00:54:23.530 --> 00:54:26.020 align:middle line:84%
So these two words
are undergoing

00:54:26.020 --> 00:54:28.360 align:middle line:84%
different types of rules,
different sets of rules.

00:54:28.360 --> 00:54:34.150 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, we had
a word, "kandun."

00:54:34.150 --> 00:54:36.220 align:middle line:90%
Here, let's do blood, "kandun."

00:54:36.220 --> 00:54:38.360 align:middle line:84%
And that, the "u,"
changed to "a."

00:54:38.360 --> 00:54:40.060 align:middle line:90%
So we've got "kanda."

00:54:40.060 --> 00:54:44.320 align:middle line:84%
But now we've got a word
which is underlying "ngaluk."

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:46.150 align:middle line:84%
And what's happening
there is that you're

00:54:46.150 --> 00:54:47.560 align:middle line:90%
dropping the final "k."

00:54:47.560 --> 00:54:48.850 align:middle line:90%
So you've got "ngalu."

00:54:48.850 --> 00:54:52.420 align:middle line:84%
But you don't change
the final "u" to an "a."

00:54:52.420 --> 00:54:54.400 align:middle line:84%
So we have these rules
that seem to apply

00:54:54.400 --> 00:54:56.560 align:middle line:90%
to certain bodies of examples.

00:54:56.560 --> 00:54:58.832 align:middle line:84%
And your suggestion,
which seems attractive,

00:54:58.832 --> 00:55:00.790 align:middle line:84%
is maybe these rules need
a little bit of work.

00:55:00.790 --> 00:55:03.250 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we need to get them to
be more specific so they only

00:55:03.250 --> 00:55:06.458 align:middle line:84%
apply to certain
kinds of things.

00:55:06.458 --> 00:55:08.250 align:middle line:84%
But here's another way
to think about this,

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:09.630 align:middle line:90%
and I'll leave you with this.

00:55:09.630 --> 00:55:12.450 align:middle line:90%


00:55:12.450 --> 00:55:16.340 align:middle line:84%
So here are the rules,
some of the rules.

00:55:16.340 --> 00:55:19.090 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, the word for
"head" starts off as "lelk."

00:55:19.090 --> 00:55:23.370 align:middle line:84%
And we add "a" because of
rule two, so we get "lelka."

00:55:23.370 --> 00:55:28.770 align:middle line:84%
But this is what we're asking,
the kind of thing we're asking.

00:55:28.770 --> 00:55:30.030 align:middle line:90%
You start with "lelk."

00:55:30.030 --> 00:55:33.240 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you apply the
rule four to get "lel,"

00:55:33.240 --> 00:55:36.510 align:middle line:84%
and then maybe rule one,
so you'd get "lelda?"

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:38.010 align:middle line:90%
Or you start with "ngaluk."

00:55:38.010 --> 00:55:40.710 align:middle line:84%
You apply rule four, so
you're getting "ngalu."

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:44.340 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you then apply
rule three to get "ngala?"

00:55:44.340 --> 00:55:46.510 align:middle line:84%
These are the kinds of
questions that we're asking.

00:55:46.510 --> 00:55:49.080 align:middle line:84%
And one kind of response to
this kind of problem that people

00:55:49.080 --> 00:55:52.350 align:middle line:84%
have classically given
is to not only have rules

00:55:52.350 --> 00:55:55.320 align:middle line:84%
but to have the rules
apply in an order, that is,

00:55:55.320 --> 00:55:56.940 align:middle line:84%
to have what's
called rule ordering.

00:55:56.940 --> 00:55:59.460 align:middle line:84%
It's as though there's an
assembly line between one

00:55:59.460 --> 00:56:01.530 align:middle line:84%
of these committees
and the other.

00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:08.340 align:middle line:84%
So rule two and rule three
happened before rule four.

00:56:08.340 --> 00:56:13.470 align:middle line:84%
So if you are
starting with "lelk,"

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:16.110 align:middle line:84%
the basic form for
"head," you first

00:56:16.110 --> 00:56:19.830 align:middle line:90%
apply rule two and get "lelka."

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:23.520 align:middle line:84%
Having applied rule two, which
adds the "a' after "lelk,"

00:56:23.520 --> 00:56:25.320 align:middle line:84%
you're not in any
danger of applying

00:56:25.320 --> 00:56:26.610 align:middle line:90%
rule three or rule four.

00:56:26.610 --> 00:56:28.658 align:middle line:84%
And rule three, never
mind, but rule four,

00:56:28.658 --> 00:56:30.450 align:middle line:84%
which would have gotten
rid of a final "k,"

00:56:30.450 --> 00:56:32.700 align:middle line:84%
doesn't apply anymore
because, well, there

00:56:32.700 --> 00:56:34.890 align:middle line:90%
isn't a final "k" anymore.

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:40.470 align:middle line:84%
Or "ngaluk," which is the
base form for story, rule four

00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:43.680 align:middle line:84%
happens after rule
two and rule three.

00:56:43.680 --> 00:56:48.120 align:middle line:84%
So rule four gets rid of the
"k' and gives you "ngalu."

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:50.370 align:middle line:84%
And you can imagine
now at this point,

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:52.710 align:middle line:84%
the rule three people are
like, hey, give us that word.

00:56:52.710 --> 00:56:53.760 align:middle line:90%
We want to get rid of that "u."

00:56:53.760 --> 00:56:54.570 align:middle line:90%
But it's too late.

00:56:54.570 --> 00:56:57.540 align:middle line:90%
Rule three has had its time.

00:56:57.540 --> 00:56:59.050 align:middle line:84%
You don't get to
apply it anymore.

00:56:59.050 --> 00:57:01.620 align:middle line:84%
So this is one way of handling
these kinds of problems,

00:57:01.620 --> 00:57:04.130 align:middle line:84%
is to see, these rules
that change things,

00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:05.880 align:middle line:84%
they're not just rules
that change things.

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:07.590 align:middle line:84%
They're rules that
apply in an order.

00:57:07.590 --> 00:57:10.830 align:middle line:84%
There's a series of
processes that you apply.

00:57:10.830 --> 00:57:15.090 align:middle line:84%
And you have to apply
them in the correct order,

00:57:15.090 --> 00:57:18.030 align:middle line:84%
and not in the incorrect order,
to get the result. Joseph?

00:57:18.030 --> 00:57:20.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So it's better to do
these like a series of rules

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.640 align:middle line:84%
that are all applied
simultaneously,

00:57:22.640 --> 00:57:24.660 align:middle line:84%
in a sequential
if/else statement.

00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:24.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Right.

00:57:24.930 --> 00:57:27.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And when you're
done, you need to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:57:27.090 --> 00:57:29.215 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That is
a way to think about this,

00:57:29.215 --> 00:57:30.007 align:middle line:90%
yes, a classic way.

00:57:30.007 --> 00:57:31.507 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about
other ways to think

00:57:31.507 --> 00:57:33.341 align:middle line:84%
about this kind of
problem, but this is one.

00:57:33.341 --> 00:57:35.570 align:middle line:90%
All right, let's stop there.

00:57:35.570 --> 00:57:39.000 align:middle line:90%